capability number/parse-int  an integer read from text in base ten
  heroes  numbers.parse_int
  go      strconv.Atoi, strconv.ParseInt (base 10)
  rust    i64::from_str, str::parse::<i64>
  python  int(str), int.__new__
  note    a sign is read, `+` included, as all three read it; nothing else is: Python's spaces around the number, its `_` between digits and its digits of other scripts (`int("١")` is 1) are `invalid` here, as in Go and Rust

capability number/parse-width  an integer read into each of the language's eight widths
  heroes  numbers.parse_i8, numbers.parse_i16, numbers.parse_i32, numbers.parse_u8, numbers.parse_u16, numbers.parse_u32, numbers.parse_u64
  go      strconv.ParseInt (bitSize 8, 16, 32, 64), strconv.ParseUint (bitSize 8, 16, 32, 64)
  rust    i8::from_str, i16::from_str, i32::from_str, u8::from_str, u16::from_str, u32::from_str, u64::from_str
  python  -
  note    a number outside the type fails `does_not_fit`, a minus before an unsigned one included, and "-0" is 0: Go and Rust call a minus there a syntax error, but the text is a number and only its type is wrong; Python has no widths, and was asked through `int` and the range

capability number/parse-int-base  an integer read from text in a given base, 2 to 36
  heroes  numbers.parse_int_base, numbers.parse_u64_base
  go      strconv.ParseInt (base 2 to 36), strconv.ParseUint (base 2 to 36)
  rust    i64::from_str_radix, u64::from_str_radix and the same of every width
  python  int(str, base)
  note    no prefix is read: Python's `int("0x1f", 16)` is 31 and here it is `invalid`, as in Go and Rust; letters of either case

capability number/parse-int-literal  an integer read with its base told by its prefix
  heroes  numbers.parse_int_literal, numbers.parse_u64_literal
  go      strconv.ParseInt (base 0), strconv.ParseUint (base 0)
  rust    -
  python  int(str, 0)
  note    the language's own spelling, so a text it reads is one the compiler reads: Python's and Go's base 0 also read `0X1F`, `0x_ff` and `00`, which the language refuses, and Go reads a lone leading zero as octal

capability number/parse-bits  the 64 bits of an integer read from text as an unsigned number
  heroes  numbers.parse_bits
  go      -
  rust    u64::from_str_radix then cast_signed
  python  -
  note    Rust reads such text into a `u64` and casts it; here the `i64` comes back whole, the bitwise operators being the `i64`'s

capability number/parse-float  a float read from decimal text, the nearest value to what the text says
  heroes  numbers.parse_float
  go      strconv.ParseFloat (bitSize 64)
  rust    f64::from_str, str::parse::<f64>
  python  float(str), float.__new__
  note    the nearest value, ties to even, for every text, checked against Python on 8,719 texts with 0 disagreeing: exact halves, 767 to 1,500 digits, both ends of range; Python's spaces, `_` and other scripts' digits are `invalid`, as in Rust; the hexadecimal form Go reads here is `parse_float_hex`

capability number/parse-f32  the same into the 32-bit float, rounded once
  heroes  numbers.parse_f32
  go      strconv.ParseFloat (bitSize 32)
  rust    f32::from_str
  python  -
  note    rounded once, where `parse_float` then `to_f32` rounds twice: "1.00000005960464477540" is 1.0000001 here and in Go, 1.0 through `f64`; 3,496 texts against Go, 0 disagreeing

capability number/parse-float-hex  a float read from hexadecimal text
  heroes  numbers.parse_float_hex
  go      strconv.ParseFloat (the 0x form)
  rust    -
  python  float.fromhex
  note    the `0x` is required, as in Go and C, where Python's `fromhex` reads "1.8p1"; a value past the largest is `inf`, where Python raises `OverflowError`; 2,266 texts against Python, 0 disagreeing

capability number/parse-bool  a boolean read from text
  heroes  numbers.parse_bool
  go      strconv.ParseBool
  rust    bool::from_str
  python  -
  note    Rust's: "true" and "false" alone, the texts `to_str` writes; Go also reads 1, t, T, TRUE, True and their falses, which a program reading a flag can test for itself

capability number/format-int  an integer written in base ten
  heroes  built-in to_str
  go      strconv.Itoa, strconv.FormatInt (base 10), strconv.FormatUint (base 10)
  rust    Display and Debug for every integer, ToString::to_string
  python  str(int), repr(int), int.__str__, int.__repr__, format (type d)
  note    base ten is the language's own `to_str`; `numbers.format_int` with base 10 writes the same

capability number/format-int-base  an integer written in a given base, 2 to 36
  heroes  numbers.format_int, numbers.format_u64
  go      strconv.FormatInt, strconv.FormatUint
  rust    LowerHex, Octal, Binary for every integer
  python  format (types x, o, b)
  note    a negative number is its sign and its magnitude, as Go and Python write it; Rust's two's complement is `format_bits`

capability number/format-int-literal  an integer written with the prefix that tells its base
  heroes  numbers.format_int_literal, numbers.format_u64_literal
  go      -
  rust    the `#` flag of LowerHex, Octal, Binary
  python  hex, oct, bin, format (the `#` option)
  note    Python's `hex`, `oct` and `bin`: the sign before the prefix, "-0xff"; `parse_int_literal` reads every one back

capability number/format-bits  the 64 bits of a signed integer written as an unsigned number
  heroes  numbers.format_bits
  go      -
  rust    LowerHex, Octal, Binary for a negative i64
  python  -
  note    Rust's `{:x}` of a negative `i64`

capability number/upper-case  a number's letters written in upper case
  heroes  strings.to_upper_ascii
  go      -
  rust    UpperHex, UpperExp
  python  format (types X, E, F, G)
  note    every text here writes letters in lower case, and `strings.to_upper_ascii` of it is Rust's `UpperHex` and `UpperExp` and Python's X, E, F and G

capability number/format-float-shortest  a float written in the fewest digits that read back as it
  heroes  numbers.format_float_shortest, numbers.format_float_exp_shortest, numbers.format_f32_shortest, numbers.format_f32_exp_shortest
  go      strconv.FormatFloat (fmt g, precision -1)
  rust    Debug for f64 and f32
  python  repr(float), str(float), float.__repr__, float.__str__
  note    the fewest digits that read back, and the nearest of them, with no exponent or in exponent form; the language's `to_str` promises only that its text reads back, and writes 2 to the -778th in 17 digits where 16 do (runtime/parts/f64.c says "shortest is not promised"); Python's `repr`, Go's `g` with -1 and Rust's `Debug` each choose between the two forms by their own thresholds, and `to_str` by its; a whole value ends ".0" as `to_str` writes it, where Go and Rust's Display write none; 11,926 cases against Go's `f` and `e` with -1, 5,963 values each written both ways, every power of two of both widths among them, 0 disagreeing

capability number/format-float-plain  the same digits written with no exponent whatever the size
  heroes  numbers.format_float_shortest
  go      strconv.FormatFloat (fmt f, precision -1)
  rust    Display for f64 and f32
  python  -
  note    Rust's `Display` and Go's `f` with -1: the shortest digits with no exponent

capability number/format-float-precision  a float written with a given count of digits after the point
  heroes  numbers.format_float
  go      strconv.FormatFloat (fmt f)
  rust    the precision of Display for f64 and f32
  python  format (types f, F), float.__format__
  note    rounded half to even from the exact value, as Python, Go and Rust do: 0.125 at 2 is "0.12" and 2.675 at 2 is "2.67"; 1,741 values against Python's `f`, 0 disagreeing

capability number/format-float-exp  a float written in exponent form
  heroes  numbers.format_float_exp
  go      strconv.FormatFloat (fmt e, E)
  rust    LowerExp for f64 and f32, and for every integer
  python  format (type e)
  note    Python's and Go's exponent, a sign and at least two digits, "1.50e+00"; Rust writes "1.50e0"; 1,741 values against Python's `e`, 0 disagreeing

capability number/format-float-general  a float written to a given count of significant digits
  heroes  numbers.format_float_general
  go      strconv.FormatFloat (fmt g, G with a precision)
  rust    -
  python  format (type g, and no type with a precision)
  note    Python's rules for `g`: the plain form where the exponent is -4 or more and under the digits, the zeros at the end left out; Go's `g` with a precision chooses its form by another threshold; 1,741 values against Python, 0 disagreeing

capability number/format-float-hex  a float written in hexadecimal
  heroes  numbers.format_float_hex
  go      strconv.FormatFloat (fmt x, X)
  rust    -
  python  float.hex
  note    C's `%a` and Go's `x` with -1: every bit and no more, "0x1.8p+1", a subnormal written from its leading one; Python's `hex` writes all thirteen digits; the exponent has no zero before it, where Go writes "p+01"; 3,790 values against Go, 0 disagreeing

capability number/format-float-binary-exponent  a float written as a decimal mantissa and a power of two
  heroes  refused: Go's `b` form writes the same exact value as `format_float_hex`, in a notation only Go reads
  go      strconv.FormatFloat (fmt b)
  rust    -
  python  -

